untry now united but where economic  and sometimes cultural differences still  exist  recent polls show two-thirds of germans  don't think the process of reunification  is complete  a task that will be up to younger  generations to accomplish in the years  to come  now let's bring in our correspondence  nina harza and potsdam and  thomas sparrow in berlin who have been  covering uh events today nina let's  start with you you've been following  president steinmeier's address  what's your take on what he said  well i thought it was a very good speech  it was very long and  president steinmeier stressed the fact  that we mustn't forget that it was a  peaceful revolution that  ultimately led to german unification  that  first brought down the berlin wall in  1989 and then helped  bring down the gdr the socialist  dictatorship as some call it and he said  of course there are still differences  there between east and west but what we  mustn't forget  is that the spirit of this peaceful  revolution is something that we need to  remember when we look at what's going on  in the world and that people want  freedom and democracy around the world  and that they're willing to sacrifice a  lot for it  and there are p people here in germany  that's what president steinmeier also  said  who are beginning to question that very  system of democracy and that they must  do everything  against those groups he was uh  quite directly referring uh to a rise in  ripening populism recently  uh we've prepared prepared a sound bite  there let's listen in  vegas how ignorant of historical fact  must those be  who would raise the black white and red  flag of the german empire of 1871  or even the battle flag in front of the  democratically elected  parliament  they're part of a tradition that doesn't  stand for our republic  or our democracy no today we stand  firmly on the bedrock of a struggle for  freedom  and a history of democracy  president steinmeier there criticizing  those waving imperial german flags in  front of the german parliament recently  thomas can you give us some background  on this  well those who were actually waving  those flags in front of the german  parliament a few weeks ago  many of them belonged to a group called  the reichsburg and the iceberg are  actually  against the german state they don't  believe even in the existence of the  german  state we saw a couple of people here a  very small group of people here at the  brandenburg gate today also waving  some of those imperial flags having  t-shirts wearing t-shirts with  conspiracy theories so they were also  probably at the  protests a few weeks ago here in in  berlin  there are other protests here in in the  brandenburg gate  today as well including uh very loud you  can probably  hear them a group of communists from  latin america that are very skeptical of  german unity and are in favor of the gdr  of the german democratic republic uh so  you can clearly see that there's a  different groups of people here in in  berlin normally this is the place where  german unity is celebrated but because  of the coronavirus pandemic  celebrations here in berlin were  cancelled  thomas 30 years after reunification  these protests are  also a sign of differences big  differences that still exist between  east and west germany  there are signs of different views on  democracy of different views on the  german  unity there's not only one narrative  about what happened  30 years ago and about how this has  developed in the last  three decades and it is important also  to stress these differences between east  and west there are differences for  example when it comes to wages when it  comes to unemployment that mostly affect  people  in the east but it's also important to  stress that three decades after german  reunification  germany has indeed progressed a lot  germany has indeed become  a very stable prosperous country at the  heart of europe and that's something  that german political leaders are very  proud about  uh let's come back to you nina you're in  potsdam where the central event was  being held earlier today what else is  planned for today  well of course thomas mentioned it the  official festivities have all been  scaled down they've been cancelled in  berlin altogether but  potsdam here has installed a big  alley where all the individual federal  states have a chance to present  themselves  in cubes i hope you can see them behind  me in the background and so people are  strolling by  and just learning about each other  essentially learning about other  regions in germany then there will be  more festivities going on at a central  square here in potsdam and at  seven o'clock german time the whole  country is supposed to sing together  this  is something which is going to happen  remotely but the main event is going to  take place in leipzig of course a very  important  place when you look at the peaceful  revolution that led to german  unification ultimately  and so the whole country can join in  virtually  as we do everything virtually most  things  virtually these days during the  coronavirus pandemic  nina has in potsdam and thomas sparrow  in berlin  thank you both  you
 

